    <body>
      <sec id="article-19023.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Umbilical arterial catheterization involves placement of a catheter into one of the neonatal umbilical arteries to obtain reliable arterial access during critical illness. The umbilical artery constricts shortly after birth but remains cannulable for several days, enabling continuous blood pressure monitoring, frequent arterial blood sampling, and the administration of fluids or medications. Proper positioning requires radiographic confirmation, and ongoing vigilance reduces the risk of thrombosis, vascular spasm, hemorrhage, infection, or organ ischemia. This procedure offers substantial clinical value but carries potentially serious complications, making precise technique and careful monitoring essential for safe use in extremely premature or unstable neonates.</p>
        <p>The learning activity guides participants through relevant anatomy, depth estimation, procedural steps, troubleshooting strategies, and evidence-based management principles for umbilical arterial catheterization. Instruction emphasizes structured communication and shared decision-making among clinicians, nurses, respiratory therapists, and radiology personnel, allowing the interprofessional team to coordinate placement, confirm catheter position, and monitor for early signs of complications. Collaborative practice enhances accuracy, promotes timely intervention, and supports safer care delivery, ultimately improving neonatal outcomes and reducing procedure-related morbidity.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify anatomic variations, clinical contraindications, and modifiable risk factors that may influence procedural success or complication likelihood.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess&#x000a0;the steps for preparation and technique of umbilical arterial catheterization and apply this knowledge to clinical scenarios.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate&#x000a0;the potential complications of umbilical arterial catheterization and how to avoid these complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional healthcare team members to provide efficient, comprehensive, and coordinated care to the&#x000a0;critically ill neonate.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=19023&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=19023">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Umbilical arterial catheterization offers direct access to the arterial system, enabling arterial blood sampling and continuous systemic blood pressure monitoring. The procedure is commonly performed in extremely premature neonates and, while technically straightforward, requires consistent practice for proficiency.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r1">[1]</xref> Complications such as infection, thrombosis, or vascular injury may occur when placement or maintenance is suboptimal.</p>
        <p>Proper catheter positioning, meticulous technique, and adherence to sterile practices are essential to reduce these risks. Continuous monitoring and skilled handling support patient safety and contribute to improved clinical outcomes. This course reviews the indications for umbilical arterial catheterization, outlines the procedural technique, and addresses potential complications associated with its use in neonatal care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>The umbilical artery originates from the anterior division of the&#x000a0;internal iliac artery. In fetal life, the artery traverses the umbilical cord, carrying deoxygenated blood from the fetus back to the&#x000a0;placenta (see <bold>Image.</bold> Infant With Umbilical Catheter). Usually, there are&#x000a0;2 umbilical arteries and&#x000a0;1 umbilical vein within the umbilical cord. The&#x000a0;2 umbilical arteries&#x000a0;have smaller, thick-walled lumens and are typically inferior to the umbilical vein, which has a larger, thin-walled lumen. The prenatal identification of an isolated single umbilical artery appears to be associated with a higher risk of small-for-gestational-age neonates and preterm delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>There are&#x000a0;2 potential positions for umbilical artery catheterization. The "high position" is at the level of T6&#x02013;T9 above the diaphragm and above the celiac artery (T12), superior mesenteric artery (T12&#x02013;L1), and renal arteries (L1). The "low position" is at L3&#x02013;L4 just above the aortic bifurcation (L4&#x02013;L5). The&#x000a0;catheter tip placed in the descending aorta at the high position is associated with lower risks of vascular&#x000a0;insufficiency, thrombus formation, and extremity cyanosis; this is usually the preferred position for umbilical artery catheterization.&#x000a0;The optimal catheter placement can be calculated using body-measurement-based or anatomic-landmark-based formulas discussed below.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>The primary indications for umbilical artery catheterization include frequent arterial blood gas measurements, blood sampling, or continuous blood pressure monitoring in extremely low-birthweight infants or critically ill neonates. Occasionally, it can be used temporarily to infuse parenteral nutrition when alternate access is unavailable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;An umbilical arterial catheter can be lifesaving in managing critically ill neonates.</p>
        <p>However, the risks and benefits must be carefully weighed before attempting the procedure, as it carries potential complications. Very low birth weight infants (with less than 1000 g body weight), preterm infants who need mechanical ventilator support, infants with hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy undergoing therapeutic hypothermia, and critically ill late preterm or term infants requiring mechanical ventilation are some of the groups of neonates who benefit from the use of an umbilical arterial catheter&#x000a0;due to the need for frequent blood sampling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications to umbilical arterial catheterization include omphalitis, necrotizing enterocolitis, omphalocele, gastroschisis, and peritonitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r6">[6]</xref> These conditions increase the risk of infection, impaired perfusion, or worsening abdominal pathology. Clinicians should avoid catheter placement in these scenarios to prevent additional complications and ensure neonatal safety.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Before performing an umbilical arterial catheterization, clinicians must gather all necessary equipment to ensure a safe, efficient, and aseptic procedure. Having the appropriate supplies readily available supports proper catheter placement and minimizes the risk of complications. Items typically required for successful catheterization include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile gown, sterile gloves, mask</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile drapes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Skin prep solution (4% chlorhexidine or povidone-iodine)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Syringe (1 mL, 3 mL, 5 mL)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>20-gauge hypodermic needle</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Two curved mosquito hemostats</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>One&#x000a0;straight mosquito hemostat</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>One full curved, nontoothed iris forceps</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>One-half curved, nontoothed iris forceps</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Two straight, toothed iris forceps</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Smooth straight forceps</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemostat needle holder</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Needle driver</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Straight iris scissors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>No. 11-blade scalpel</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A 3-0 silk suture with a curved cutting needle</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Umbilical&#x000a0;tape 15 in</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Measuring tape</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Single lumen umbilical catheter:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5-Fr gauge for infants weighing &#x0003e;1500 g</p></list-item><list-item><p>3.5-Fr gauge for infants weighing &#x0003c;1500 g</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Three-way stopcock</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous tubing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile&#x000a0;heparinized&#x000a0;flush</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multiple 4 x 4-inch gauze sponges&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>A clinician adequately trained should perform the procedure. Secondary personnel, such as a nurse, should also be present to help monitor the patient throughout the process.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Proper preparation is essential to ensure safe and effective umbilical artery catheterization. Key steps, including determining the appropriate catheter depth, help minimize complications and optimize placement in critically ill neonates.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Depth of Catheterization</bold>
</p>
        <p>After determining the need for an umbilical arterial catheter, informed consent must be obtained in accordance with the local institutional policy.&#x000a0;The ideal position of the catheter tip should be at the "high position" in the descending aorta, above the origins of the celiac, mesenteric, and renal arteries, to avoid occluding these vessels. This high position, in which the catheter tip lies above the diaphragm between T6 and T9, is preferred over the low position at L3 and L4 because it is associated with fewer complications and a reduced risk of thrombosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Several methods are used to determine the depth of insertion of the umbilical artery catheter. The Shukla and Wright formulas take into account the neonate's weight. The Shukla formula is as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Umbilical Artery Catheter Depth (cm) = (birth weight in kg x 3) + 9&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The Wright formula is as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Umbilical Artery Catheter Depth (cm) = (birth weight kg x 4) + 7&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Gupta described an approach relying on anatomical landmarks to determine the depth of insertion rather than body weight. This approach relies on the distances from the umbilicus to nipple (UN) and from the umbilicus to the symphysis pubis (USp). This was thought to provide more accurate estimates of insertion length than body-weight-based formulas. The Gupta formula is as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Umbilical Artery Catheter Depth (cm) = (UN - 1 cm) + 2 USp&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Other methods for estimating the depth of insertion are also described.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Successful umbilical artery catheterization requires a systematic, precise technique to ensure accurate placement and minimize complications. Clinicians must follow evidence-based procedural steps, maintain aseptic conditions, and carefully handle the catheter to protect the fragile vasculature of critically ill neonates. Attention to detail throughout the procedure is essential for optimal outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Catheter Placement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Before starting the procedure, the&#x000a0;neonate must be restrained with soft arm and leg restraints to minimize movement. The neonate can be placed supine on a radiant warmer, with the head positioned at the top of the warmer to avoid hypothermia during the procedure. The umbilical cord and the surrounding skin are cleaned with an antiseptic solution. The catheter is prepared in a sterile fashion by connecting a 3-way stopcock to the catheter, connecting a 5-mL syringe to each port of the stopcock, and flushing the system with heparinized solution (0.45% sodium chloride plus 1:1 heparin) to ensure no air bubbles are present. The stopcock is then turned off to the catheter. The assistant holds up the&#x000a0;umbilical&#x000a0;cord with the cord clamp so that the physician/advanced practice provider&#x000a0;can clean the cord and the skin 3 to 5 cm around the cord base with 4% chlorhexidine or povidone-iodine solution.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The abdomen&#x000a0;is then draped, and an umbilical tie is placed around the cord. (Tie a square knot tight enough to minimize blood loss but loose enough to allow catheters to be passed easily through the vessels.) The cord is then cut horizontally with an 11-blade scalpel, 1 to 1.5 cm from the skin. The cord stump is grasped, and traction is applied.</p>
        <p>Identify the vessels of the cord:&#x000a0;2 small muscular arteries with a thick lumen and&#x000a0;1 thin-walled umbilical vein. The lumen of&#x000a0;1 of the arteries is probed and gently dilated with the curved iris forceps. The tips of the iris forceps are used to spread open and dilate the lumen for 15 to 30 seconds. Once the artery is dilated, the catheter is then grasped approximately 1 cm from the tip with the curved iris forceps, or between the thumb and forefinger, and inserted into the lumen of the dilated artery.</p>
        <p>Advance the catheter into the lumen to 2 cm deep, then remove the iris forceps.&#x000a0;Continue advancing the catheter to 4 to 5 cm and aspirate to verify the lumen position. If blood is aspirated, the catheter is within the lumen. Clear the catheter by injecting 0.5 mL of heparinized solution.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Repeated probing or excessive pressure can result in vessel perforation. If unsuccessful, catheterization of the other artery must be attempted. Once the catheter is in place, an abdominal x-ray is performed to confirm proper positioning between the T6 and T9 vertebrae. On imaging, the catheter should be visualized entering the umbilical cord, diving inferiorly to connect with the internal iliac artery, then curving cephalad to enter the aorta between T6 and T9. The catheter can be retracted if needed, but it should never be advanced once it is secured due to the risk of introducing infection. However, the use of ultrasound guidance for umbilical catheter tip placement has become increasingly prevalent due to its demonstrated accuracy, cost-effectiveness, and enhanced safety profile.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Once the position is confirmed, the catheter is secured using purse-string sutures around the base of the cord, and the tie is removed.&#x000a0;Infusion of a heparinized solution must be started as soon as the catheter tip is verified to be in an appropriate position. Connect the arterial pressure transducer to verify the presence of proper arterial waveforms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Catheter Removal</bold>
</p>
        <p>Once the catheter is ready to be removed, stop any infusions, cut the suture around the base of the cord, and pull the catheter back by 1 cm to 2 cm. Wait approximately 5 to 10 minutes to allow for constriction of the umbilical artery. Once bleeding has stopped, remove the rest of the catheter. Place the neonate in the supine position for 30 to 60 minutes after removal to monitor for any bleeding.&#x000a0;If bleeding occurs during catheter removal, apply pressure with umbilical tape around the base of the cord.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Catheter Troubleshooting</bold>
</p>
        <p>Take the time to dilate the vessel, as this increases the likelihood of success and reduces the risk of perforating it or creating a false track. If there is any resistance to advancing the catheter, gentle, steady pressure must be applied. If there is a "popping" sensation during catheter advancement, it may indicate&#x000a0;that the catheter has exited the lumen and created a false track. If this occurs, remove the catheter and use the second vessel for catheterization. If vessel spasm happens during insertion, use 0.5 mL of 2% lidocaine without epinephrine as a vasodilator, injecting it&#x000a0;into the vessel while applying constant pressure until the artery is appropriately dilated. Do not advance the catheter once it is securely placed in the appropriate location, as this can significantly increase the risk of infection by introducing contaminated portions of the catheter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Thromboembolism is a major concern with the use of umbilical arterial catheters. Occlusion of the mesenteric arteries can result in gut ischemia, bowel infarction, and necrotizing enterocolitis. Renal artery involvement can lead to hypertension or acute renal failure. More distal vascular accidents can result in an ischemic injury to the back, buttocks, and lower extremities. Remove the catheter if there is any sign of vascular compromise. Other complications, such as vessel perforation, peritoneal perforation, bladder injury, and false aneurysm, can occur during catheter insertion.</p>
        <p>Catheter-associated infection and bleeding are other frequently associated complications. Umbilical artery catheters should be kept in place for no more than 5 days due to the risk of infection. Refractory hypoglycemia is a rare complication associated with high-position umbilical artery catheters whose tip is near the celiac and mesenteric arteries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;There is a theoretical concern about the risk of necrotizing enterocolitis in infants receiving enteral feedings while an umbilical arterial catheter is in place. However, there is no evidence supporting this, and withholding enteral feeds while an infant has an umbilical arterial catheter is no longer recommended.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>An umbilical arterial catheter can be lifesaving in caring for critically ill neonates. However, the risks and benefits must be carefully weighed before attempting the procedure, as it carries potential complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r21">[21]</xref> Extremely low birth weight infants (&#x0003c;1000 g), preterm infants&#x000a0;who require mechanical ventilatory support, infants with hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy undergoing therapeutic hypothermia, and critically ill late preterm or term infants requiring mechanical ventilation are some of the groups of neonates who&#x000a0;may benefit from the use of umbilical arterial&#x000a0;catheterization. Adhering to guidelines that standardize&#x000a0;the use of umbilical catheters in neonates is imperative.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>An interprofessional healthcare team approach is essential for the successful insertion and maintenance of umbilical arterial catheterization. This team will include advanced clinicians, specialists, mid-level practitioners, nursing staff, and surgical assistants, all with specialized neonatal care training. Communication and coordination between the clinicians/advanced practitioners and nurses are very important. The necessity of the catheter must be evaluated daily by the healthcare team, with nursing staff reporting any findings observed during care. The catheter must be removed when no longer necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Continuation of the umbilical arterial catheter beyond 5 days is not recommended due to the risk of infection and thrombosis associated with its prolonged use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-19023.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s13" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>Umbilical artery catheterization requires interprofessional collaboration. The nursing team plays a vital role in ensuring consent has been obtained, preparing the catheterization kit, and ensuring that the line is not used until an abdominal x-ray verifies the catheter's appropriate placement.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-19023.s14" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring</title>
        <p>Once the umbilical artery catheter has been placed, the nursing teams ensure all infusions are sterile and dressings are changed regularly. If any bleeding or concerns regarding the line's functioning occur, the nursing team should immediately alert the clinicians.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
